menu "Cyphal node (cyphal)"

    config CYPHAL_DEFAULT_NAME
        string "The node's name in uavcan.node.GetInfo, until `cyphal name` changes it"
        default "node"
        help
            1 to 50 characters of a-z, 0-9, _ and ., with no empty part and no part starting with
            a digit (cyphal_name_valid(), cyphal_util.h). Checked at start: a default that breaks
            the rules is replaced by "node", with a warning. A firmware sets its own in its
            sdkconfig.defaults.

    config CYPHAL_NAME_REGISTER
        string "The register that holds the node's name"
        default "node.name"
        help
            The name uavcan.register.Access reads and writes the node's name by (the setting
            `cyphal name` changes): a register name by the standard's rules, a-z, 0-9, _ and .,
            with at least one dot. Not uavcan.node.description, which is a free-text note that
            doesn't change what GetInfo reports. A firmware sets its own in its
            sdkconfig.defaults.

    config CYPHAL_UNIQUE_ID_PREFIX
        string "The unique ID's first 8 bytes: the firmware's name"
        default "esp-node"
        help
            Exactly 8 characters. The unique ID is these 8 bytes, two zero bytes and the chip's
            base MAC address (cyphal_unique_id(), cyphal_util.h). An allocator gives a node-ID
            by unique ID, so changing this gives every board a new node-ID: a firmware pins its
            own in its sdkconfig.defaults.

    config CYPHAL_HARDWARE_VERSION_MAJOR
        int "The hardware version GetInfo reports: major"
        default 0
        range 0 255

    config CYPHAL_HARDWARE_VERSION_MINOR
        int "The hardware version GetInfo reports: minor"
        default 0
        range 0 255

    config CYPHAL_HEAP_SIZE
        int "libcanard's heap (bytes, in PSRAM)"
        default 32768
        range 8192 131072
        help
            A heap of its own for libcanard, reserved at the node's first start and kept, in PSRAM
            when there is any. Each start begins it empty. It holds the transmit queue (at most
            160 frames, 84 bytes each with the allocator's overhead: 13.4 KB; a port list is 22
            frames), the receive sessions (36 bytes for each node and service that has sent the
            node a request: 4.6 KB for 127 nodes on one service; and, while it waits for a
            node-ID, one for each allocator that answers) and each transfer as it is reassembled,
            at its type's extent (a register request, 515 bytes). Measured on holo-player-fw
            (ESP32-S3) 2026-11-20 with the
            register and command services and the port list: 1,932 bytes at most (a port list
            waiting to go), through 500 GetInfo calls back to back and the whole hardware suite;
            2,016 alone on the bus for 10 minutes, where every transfer waits out its second. With
            the holo's ports (a Status of 13 frames once a second or four, Axes, setpoints at 50 a
            second, Motion and Light), the same day: 2,124 at most over 10 minutes of motions,
            setpoint bursts and lights during a clip, 2,088 over the holo's tests, and 4,460 alone on
            the bus for 10 minutes, where each Status waits out its second too.
            `cyphal` shows how much it has held at most.

    config CYPHAL_TASK_PRIORITY
        int "The node task's priority"
        default 5
        range 1 24
        help
            The task reads the controller's receive queue (CONFIG_CANBUS_RX_QUEUE_LEN frames) and
            keeps the heartbeat on time, so it should not wait behind a task that keeps a core
            busy (holo-player-fw's video player, a display's render task).

    config CYPHAL_TASK_CORE
        int "The core the node task runs on"
        default 0
        range 0 1

    config CYPHAL_TASK_STACK
        int "The node task's stack (bytes)"
        default 3072
        help
            In internal RAM, while the node runs. Measured on holo-player-fw (ESP32-S3)
            2026-11-20 with `cyphal`'s report of the
            stack never used, over 500 GetInfo calls back to back, a clip, an update written over
            Wi-Fi, board restarts and every kind of collision stop (the listen, running, and in the
            middle of a response): 1,312 bytes used in most runs, 1,636 at most (924 never used),
            after a log line (going online, or the collision warning). With plug-and-play, the
            same day: 1,756 at most (804 never used), just after an allocation, whose log line is
            written from inside the transmit path; the same after a 15-minute soak with a clip, the
            LED ring and the web app. Log lines are the deepest calls; the spread is
            interrupts' register spills. The register and command services deserialize and
            serialize on it too (their structs are in PSRAM, not on it), hence 3,072: measured
            2026-11-20 over the whole hardware suite, 1,552 bytes used at most (1,520 never used),
            in the test of a collision in the middle of a response. The holo's requests are decoded
            and its setpoints copied here too: 1,356 used at most (1,716 never used) over the holo's
            tests and 10 minutes of them during a clip.

    config CYPHAL_WORKER_STACK
        int "The node's worker task's stack (bytes)"
        default 4608
        help
            In internal RAM, from the node's first start on. The worker does what the node's task
            may not wait for: a register written to NVS, a restart, a factory reset (NVS), and
            identify, unless the application says identify can take long (cyphal_app_t.identify_slow,
            as holo-player-fw does), and the application's requests and poll. On holo-player-fw
            (ESP32-S3) it also runs the holo's Motion (the HTTP API's motion code), Light and
            Ring, carries out its setpoints, and builds its Status, Event (a happening's JSON parsed)
            and Axes, reading the screen without waiting for it (the application's poll). Work that
            can take long (an image shown, which decodes it; a scene applied; identify) goes to the
            second worker instead (CONFIG_CYPHAL_SLOW_STACK). Measured 2026-11-21 on 6,144 bytes over
            the holo's, the scenes', screen and ring, register and command tests and 5 minutes of the
            holo's load test: 2,088 bytes used at most (2,012 by a factory reset's NVS writes, logged
            before the restart). 4,608 leaves 2.5 KB of that unused.
            (Earlier, with identify on the worker: 2,844; while Motion built the route's JSON: 3,052.)

    config CYPHAL_WORKER_PRIORITY
        int "The node's worker task's priority"
        default 5
        range 1 24
        help
            Below the node's task (CONFIG_CYPHAL_TASK_PRIORITY), which must never wait for it
            (holo-player-fw's host link task, which runs the same kind of work, is at 5).

    config CYPHAL_SLOW_STACK
        int "The node's second worker's stack (bytes)"
        default 7680
        help
            In internal RAM, from the node's first start on, and only when the application has work
            that can take long (cyphal_handler_t.slow, cyphal_app_t.identify_slow): on
            holo-player-fw r2.holo.Scene and Screen, which show a file (an image is decoded on the task that shows
            it), and identify, which applies a scene. It can't be in PSRAM there, as the kit's servo
            task's can (SERVO_TASK_STACK_IN_PSRAM): that task reads LittleFS (the file shown) and NVS (the
            scene), and a flash access turns the cache off, which leaves PSRAM unreachable here (no
            XIP from PSRAM: CONFIG_SPIRAM_XIP_FROM_PSRAM and CONFIG_SPIRAM_FETCH_INSTRUCTIONS are off),
            a stack in it included; servo never touches flash. Measured 2026-11-21 on 8,192 bytes over
            the Scene, Screen and event tests and every still on the board through Screen (PNGs of
            each kind, an interlaced and a worst-case PNG, JPEGs, clips, GIFs, refusals, scenes that
            show them, identify): 5,708 bytes used at most, by a palette PNG; 7,680 leaves 1.9 KB of
            that unused. The host link's task (CONFIG_HOSTLINK_TASK_STACK) and the web server's run
            the same routes on 10 KB; the host link's doesn't run while its pins are the node's.

    config CYPHAL_SLOW_PRIORITY
        int "The node's second worker's priority"
        default 4
        range 1 24
        help
            Below the worker (CONFIG_CYPHAL_WORKER_PRIORITY): slow work here (an image decoding, on
            holo-player-fw) doesn't keep the worker's setpoints and published messages waiting.

endmenu
